  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday night through Friday)\nIssued at 458 AM EDT Sat May 16 2026\n\nBy Monday morning...GA will be situated on the western periphery of \nthe Atlantic ridge keeping the region in moist southerly flow. This \nsummer-like pattern sticks around during the early week with minimal \nprecip chances (20% to 30%) and warm conditions (middle 80s to lower \n90s) Monday and Tuesday. The ridge centered off the Carolina coast \nslides slowly east out to sea allowing a more sustained increase in \nrain chances/coverage to push in through the end of the week. \n\nBy Wednesday, a cold front will be making progress southeastward \ntoward the forecast area, providing a bigger impetus for increased \nrain chances. While timing is inexact at this time, this front looks \nto slowly push across the state from Wednesday through Friday, with \nPoPs maximized for days 5 to 7. While any rainfall is certainly \nappreciated, this will not be a \"drought-busting\" type event by any \nmeans.\n\nTemperatures remain primarily 5-10 degrees above normal with highs \nlargely in the upper 80s to lower 90s and lows in the 60s.",
